    <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em;">TTR200 RAIL-MOUNT TEMPERATURE TRANSMITTER | CI/TTR200-EN REV. C</div>

    <h2 class="section-title">7 Electrical connections</h2>
    <h3 class="section-title">General</h3>
    <p>In case of corresponding order the transmitter is ready for operation after mounting and installation of the connections. The parameters are set at the factory.</p>
    <p>The connected lines must be checked for firm seating. Only firmly seated lines ensure full functionality.</p>

    <h3 class="section-title">Checks prior to commissioning</h3>
    <p>The following points must be checked before commissioning the device:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><span data-field="check_points.electrical">{{ check_points.electrical }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="check_points.ambient">{{ check_points.ambient }}</span></li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="section-title">Communication</h3>
    <div class="note">
        The HART® protocol is an unsecured protocol, as such the intended application should be assessed to ensure that these protocols are suitable before implementation.
    </div>
    <p>Communication with the transmitter takes place using the HART® protocol. The communication signal is modulated onto both wires of the signal line in accordance with the HART FSK 'Physical Layer' specification.</p>
    <p>The electrical connection is provided at the + and - terminals of the transmitter or by the power supply cable that is installed on-site. The advantage of this is that remote configuration is possible with supply units that are part of the industrial plant.</p>

    <h3 class="section-title">Parameterization of the device</h3>
    <div class="note">
        The device does not have operating elements for parameterization on site.
        Parameterization takes place via the HART interface.
    </div>
    <p>Parameterization of the device takes place via standard HART® tools. These include:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><span data-field="parameterization_tools.abb_handheld">{{ parameterization_tools.abb_handheld }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="parameterization_tools.abb_asset_vision">{{ parameterization_tools.abb_asset_vision }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="parameterization_tools.abb_asset_control">{{ parameterization_tools.abb_asset_control }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="parameterization_tools.abb_field_info">{{ parameterization_tools.abb_field_info }}</span></li>
        <li><span data-field="parameterization_tools.other_tools">{{ parameterization_tools.other_tools }}</span></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note">
        Not all tools and frame applications support DTMs or EDDs at the same level. In particular, optional or advanced EDD / DTM functions may potentially not be available on all tools.
        ABB provides frame applications supporting the full range of functions and performance.
    </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div style="text-align: left; font-size: 0.9em;">22</div>
    <div style="text-align: right; font-size: 0.9em;">TTR200 RAIL-MOUNT TEMPERATURE TRANSMITTER | CI/TTR200-EN REV. C</div>

    <h2 class="section-title">8 Commissioning</h2>

    <h3 class="section-title">Basic Setup</h3>
    <p>Sensor error adjustment (DTM adjustment function)</p>
    <p>Sensor error adjustment can be performed in the DTM by navigating to the menu path Device / Calibration.</p>
    <p>The sensor error adjustment is performed to compensate for the temperature / Trim low using a water quench or oven. It is important to make sure the temperature is balanced and stable.</p>

    <p>In the DTM, check that the proper adjustment temperature has been selected for the sensor before adjusting it. Based on the comparison of the adjustment temperature and the transmitter, which is available after linearization in the form of HART temperature information, the transmitter calculates the temperature deviation resulting from the sensor error.</p>

    <p>A pure sensor offset error can be corrected via the calibration function 'Set lower range value' or the adjustment function 'Trim low'.</p>
    <p>By contrast, if the error is not a pure sensor offset error, it can only be corrected using two-point adjustment or two-point calibration.</p>

    <h3 class="section-title">D / A analog output adjustment (4 mA and 20 mA-Trim)</h3>
    <p>D / A analog output adjustment is used to compensate for errors in the current input of the higher-level system. D/A analog output adjustment is used to modify the loop current that the desired value is displayed in the higher-level system.</p>

    <p>Error compensation for the higher-level system is possible at the lower range value with 4 mA and / or 20 mA (single-point error correction: offset or two-point error correction: offset + linear).</p>

    <p>The D / A analog output adjustment can be accessed in the DTM via the menu path Device / Calibration.</p>
    <p>Prior to analog adjustment, it is necessary to determine the loop current values based on iterative entry of current values in simulation mode; the higher-level I/O system displays exactly 4.00 mA or the lower range limit temperature, and 20.00 mA or the upper range limit temperature. The current loop values must be measured using an ammeter and recorded.</p>

    <p>The lower range limit value or 4.000 mA should then be simulated in DTM to obtain the corresponding voltage value for the sensor simulation. Following this, the iteratively calculated current values are entered into the DTM to adjust the higher-level system displays exactly 4.00 mA or the lower range limit temperature, and 20.00 mA or the upper range limit temperature. The current loop values must be measured using an ammeter and recorded.</p>

    <p>After this correction, the AD converter error of the higher-level system is corrected by the D/A converter of the transmitter for the higher-level system, the value of the analog 4 to 20 mA output signal and the digital HART signal now match.</p>

    <p>The adjustment should be repeated when connecting the transmitter to another input of a higher-level system.</p>
</div>

